1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,110
every time your browser or your app sends a

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:06,220
request to a server, the server sends back a

3
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:08,334
response and the first line of that response

4
00:00:08,334 --> 00:00:10,230
has a three-digit number

5
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:12,730
the number is the HTTP status code

6
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:14,917
in this case here, 200 is the status code

7
00:00:14,917 --> 00:00:16,603
that OK is the reason phrase

8
00:00:16,603 --> 00:00:18,643
the job of the status code is to tell you

9
00:00:18,643 --> 00:00:21,513
what happened while the body gives you the

10
00:00:21,513 --> 00:00:23,333
detail and you can already tell a lot from

11
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:25,593
the first digit of the number

12
00:00:25,593 --> 00:00:28,203
HTTP status codes are grouped into five

13
00:00:28,203 --> 00:00:30,183
classes the first digit of the three digit

14
00:00:30,183 --> 00:00:32,283
number tells you which class it is

15
00:00:32,283 --> 00:00:35,013
the easiest way to think of them is to think

16
00:00:35,013 --> 00:00:37,763
of asking for a book at a bookshop counter

17
00:00:37,763 --> 00:00:40,523
1xx means, hold on, I'm working on it

18
00:00:40,523 --> 00:00:42,333
like the attendant telling you, hey, give me

19
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:43,403
a second, let me check

20
00:00:43,403 --> 00:00:45,037
2xx means success

21
00:00:45,037 --> 00:00:46,617
I've got your book, right?

22
00:00:46,617 --> 00:00:47,697
yeah, it is

23
00:00:47,697 --> 00:00:49,857
and 3xx means redirection

24
00:00:49,857 --> 00:00:52,587
we moved that book to our other branch, go

25
00:00:52,587 --> 00:00:54,597
there 4xx means client error

26
00:00:54,597 --> 00:00:56,717
something is wrong with what you asked for

27
00:00:56,717 --> 00:00:58,097
maybe you asked for a book that doesn't

28
00:00:58,097 --> 00:00:59,627
exist or you didn't show your Bookshop

29
00:00:59,627 --> 00:01:02,117
membership card 5xx means server error

30
00:01:02,117 --> 00:01:04,157
your request was fine, but something broke

31
00:01:04,157 --> 00:01:04,797
on our end

32
00:01:04,797 --> 00:01:06,837
our bookstore computer system went down,

33
00:01:06,837 --> 00:01:07,857
whatever it is

34
00:01:07,857 --> 00:01:10,287
so when something fails or succeeds, look at

35
00:01:10,287 --> 00:01:12,567
the first digit of this three-digit number

36
00:01:12,567 --> 00:01:14,547
first if we start with a four, we start by

37
00:01:14,547 --> 00:01:16,357
checking the request the client sends

38
00:01:16,357 --> 00:01:18,027
if we start with a five, we start by

39
00:01:18,027 --> 00:01:18,757
checking the server

40
00:01:18,757 --> 00:01:20,327
now we'll go through each of them, each of

41
00:01:20,327 --> 00:01:22,147
this group using our bookstore API

42
00:01:22,147 --> 00:01:24,907
the 1xx status codes, you rarely see this

43
00:01:24,907 --> 00:01:27,484
for yourself you might see 101 Switching

44
00:01:27,484 --> 00:01:30,197
Protocols when a connection upgrades to a

45
00:01:30,197 --> 00:01:33,677
WebSocket, but your day-to-day API is mostly

46
00:01:33,677 --> 00:01:36,397
2xx to 5xx HTTP status codes

47
00:01:36,397 --> 00:01:39,197
the 2xx status codes mean success

48
00:01:39,197 --> 00:01:42,067
and there are three that you will use all of

49
00:01:42,067 --> 00:01:44,677
the time 200 OK means your request worked

50
00:01:44,677 --> 00:01:45,637
here's the result

51
00:01:45,637 --> 00:01:49,160
so GET book 42 returns 200 OK with the book

52
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:51,560
201 Created means the request worked and

53
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:52,730
something new was created

54
00:01:52,730 --> 00:01:55,310
so when you POST books to add a new book,

55
00:01:55,310 --> 00:01:57,230
the right answer is 201

56
00:01:57,230 --> 00:01:58,810
you can also add a Location header that

57
00:01:58,810 --> 00:01:59,670
points to the new book

58
00:01:59,670 --> 00:02:02,800
204 No Content, means that your request

59
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:04,590
worked, but there's nothing to send back

60
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:07,030
usually we get a 204 a lot with delete

61
00:02:07,030 --> 00:02:08,880
for example, in DELETE book 42, there is

62
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:09,910
nothing left to show

63
00:02:09,910 --> 00:02:13,110
so you return 204 with an empty body

64
00:02:13,110 --> 00:02:16,521
the 3xx HTTP status codes are redirects

65
00:02:16,521 --> 00:02:20,620
they tell the client that what it wants is

66
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:21,417
at a different

67
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:23,933
address so the new address goes in the

68
00:02:23,933 --> 00:02:25,893
Location header there are four common 3xx

69
00:02:25,893 --> 00:02:28,003
HTTP status codes that you will see

70
00:02:28,003 --> 00:02:29,263
and to pick the right one, you just need to

71
00:02:29,263 --> 00:02:29,983
answer two questions

72
00:02:29,983 --> 00:02:31,923
question one, is the move permanent or

73
00:02:31,923 --> 00:02:34,183
temporary? if a bookshop moves that book to

74
00:02:34,183 --> 00:02:37,463
the other branch for good, that's a 301 or a

75
00:02:37,463 --> 00:02:39,793
308 if the book is only there for now and

76
00:02:39,793 --> 00:02:42,223
it's coming back, that's a 302 or 307

77
00:02:42,223 --> 00:02:44,893
the second question, does the request stay

78
00:02:44,893 --> 00:02:46,443
the same when the client follows the

79
00:02:46,443 --> 00:02:48,713
redirect so you come to the counter to hand

80
00:02:48,713 --> 00:02:50,523
in an order form, and they send it to the

81
00:02:50,523 --> 00:02:53,263
other branch with 307 or 308, you take the

82
00:02:53,263 --> 00:02:55,163
same form to the other branch, and you hand

83
00:02:55,163 --> 00:02:55,843
it in there

84
00:02:55,843 --> 00:02:58,393
with 301 and 302, the browser is allowed to

85
00:02:58,393 --> 00:02:59,783
switch from POST to GET

86
00:02:59,783 --> 00:03:01,863
that so for normal web pages, 301 and 302

87
00:03:01,863 --> 00:03:03,933
are fine if you're redirecting an API where

88
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:06,613
POST requests matter, I would use 307 and

89
00:03:06,613 --> 00:03:10,133
308 now the 4xx status codes are client

90
00:03:10,133 --> 00:03:11,823
errors they mean the system thinks something

91
00:03:11,823 --> 00:03:14,073
is wrong with the request and they are the

92
00:03:14,073 --> 00:03:15,343
ones you would debug the most

93
00:03:15,343 --> 00:03:17,743
let's start with 400 and 422 because they

94
00:03:17,743 --> 00:03:19,103
sound similar 400 Bad Request means the

95
00:03:19,103 --> 00:03:21,023
server can't read your request properly

96
00:03:21,023 --> 00:03:23,063
like broken JSON with a missing bracket

97
00:03:23,063 --> 00:03:25,583
422 Unprocessable Content means that the

98
00:03:25,583 --> 00:03:27,283
server could read the request just fine, but

99
00:03:27,283 --> 00:03:28,823
the data inside does not make sense

100
00:03:28,823 --> 00:03:31,553
if you send a bookstore a new book with no

101
00:03:31,553 --> 00:03:34,093
title or something like that, you get 422

102
00:03:34,093 --> 00:03:36,963
some APIs send 400 for validation errors too

103
00:03:36,963 --> 00:03:39,023
I think 422 is clearer because it tells the

104
00:03:39,023 --> 00:03:41,173
front end that the format was fine and data

105
00:03:41,173 --> 00:03:42,583
is what needs fixing

106
00:03:42,583 --> 00:03:44,503
404 Not Found means that there's nothing in

107
00:03:44,503 --> 00:03:47,673
the address so if I send GET book 9999 and

108
00:03:47,673 --> 00:03:49,383
there's no book 9999, the book does not

109
00:03:49,383 --> 00:03:51,983
exist so this client gets a 404 Not Found

110
00:03:51,983 --> 00:03:53,503
you probably send a 404 yourself

111
00:03:53,503 --> 00:03:56,383
410 Gone is a stronger version of 404

112
00:03:56,383 --> 00:03:58,383
It means that this resource was here, it was

113
00:03:58,383 --> 00:04:00,103
removed on purpose and it should not be back

114
00:04:00,103 --> 00:04:01,773
like a book that you've taken out of the

115
00:04:01,773 --> 00:04:03,213
catalog for good

116
00:04:03,213 --> 00:04:06,175
in practice, most APIs use 404, but Google's

117
00:04:06,175 --> 00:04:09,583
docs say it treats 404 and 410 the same way

118
00:04:09,583 --> 00:04:11,523
so you use 410 Gone when you want to be

119
00:04:11,523 --> 00:04:13,463
clear that something is gone for good

120
00:04:13,463 --> 00:04:16,773
409 Conflict means the request clashed with

121
00:04:16,773 --> 00:04:18,613
the current state of the thing that you're

122
00:04:18,613 --> 00:04:21,103
changing say two admins open book 42 at the

123
00:04:21,103 --> 00:04:23,183
same time admin A changes the price and save

124
00:04:23,183 --> 00:04:24,983
admin B is still looking at the old version

125
00:04:24,983 --> 00:04:26,663
they change the title and save

126
00:04:26,663 --> 00:04:28,543
and that save will put the old price back

127
00:04:28,543 --> 00:04:30,803
so if the API keeps track of versions, It

128
00:04:30,803 --> 00:04:32,853
can reply with 409 Conflict to admin B

129
00:04:32,853 --> 00:04:35,183
instead, so that it can reload and try again

130
00:04:35,183 --> 00:04:37,123
429, Too Many Requests means you have been

131
00:04:37,123 --> 00:04:39,493
rate limited It means you have sent too many

132
00:04:39,493 --> 00:04:41,363
requests in a short time and is asking you

133
00:04:41,363 --> 00:04:42,083
to slow down

134
00:04:42,083 --> 00:04:44,023
now, 401 versus 403

135
00:04:44,023 --> 00:04:46,563
401 versus 403 are two status codes that

136
00:04:46,563 --> 00:04:48,543
people mix up together a lot

137
00:04:48,543 --> 00:04:51,273
say our bookstore has an admin route, DELETE

138
00:04:51,273 --> 00:04:53,903
book 42 only admins can delete book

139
00:04:53,903 --> 00:04:55,383
case one, now let's look at two scenarios

140
00:04:55,383 --> 00:04:57,053
case one, a request comes in with no token

141
00:04:57,053 --> 00:04:58,343
at all, or the token has expired

142
00:04:58,343 --> 00:05:00,278
the server has no idea who's sending this

143
00:05:00,278 --> 00:05:03,043
request that is a 401 Unauthorized

144
00:05:03,043 --> 00:05:04,723
case two, the request has a valid token

145
00:05:04,723 --> 00:05:06,443
so the server knows exactly who it is

146
00:05:06,443 --> 00:05:08,363
it's a customer, and customers cannot delete

147
00:05:08,363 --> 00:05:09,923
book that is a 403 Forbidden

148
00:05:09,923 --> 00:05:11,843
so two different cases, right?

149
00:05:11,843 --> 00:05:13,643
it's like when you have a hotel keycard

150
00:05:13,643 --> 00:05:15,363
401 is when you don't even have a keycard at

151
00:05:15,363 --> 00:05:16,683
all or your card doesn't work

152
00:05:16,683 --> 00:05:18,743
403 is when your card works fine, but it's

153
00:05:18,743 --> 00:05:20,183
for another room and you're trying to enter

154
00:05:20,183 --> 00:05:22,283
another room so 401 is kind of who are you?

155
00:05:22,283 --> 00:05:24,123
while 403 is kind of, I know who you are,

156
00:05:24,123 --> 00:05:25,363
but you can't do this

157
00:05:25,363 --> 00:05:27,033
the part that confuses people mostly is the

158
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:28,913
name 401 is called Unauthorized

159
00:05:28,913 --> 00:05:30,553
and meanwhile, it's really about

160
00:05:30,553 --> 00:05:33,203
authentication 403, on the other hand, is

161
00:05:33,203 --> 00:05:34,643
the one that is about permission

162
00:05:34,643 --> 00:05:37,033
on a 401, the right move is to send the user

163
00:05:37,033 --> 00:05:38,803
to login or refresh their token

164
00:05:38,803 --> 00:05:41,003
on a 403, logging in again won't help

165
00:05:41,003 --> 00:05:42,683
because the server already knows who they

166
00:05:42,683 --> 00:05:44,283
are you just need to show them a message

167
00:05:44,283 --> 00:05:46,083
that they don't have the access to do this

168
00:05:46,083 --> 00:05:48,403
sometimes a server will return 404 instead

169
00:05:48,403 --> 00:05:50,403
of 403 on purpose just to hide that

170
00:05:50,403 --> 00:05:52,653
something exists for example, GitHub's API

171
00:05:52,653 --> 00:05:54,163
does this if you ask for a private

172
00:05:54,163 --> 00:05:56,313
repository that you don't have access to,

173
00:05:56,313 --> 00:05:59,303
you get a 404 so that GitHub doesn't confirm

174
00:05:59,303 --> 00:06:01,223
to you that the repository exists

175
00:06:01,223 --> 00:06:02,923
you shouldn't have access to it, and you

176
00:06:02,923 --> 00:06:04,923
shouldn't even know that it exists

177
00:06:04,923 --> 00:06:07,683
now, the 5xx status codes are server errors

178
00:06:07,683 --> 00:06:09,853
the requests look fine, but the server

179
00:06:09,853 --> 00:06:11,123
failed to handle it

180
00:06:11,123 --> 00:06:14,403
500 Internal Server Error is the general one

181
00:06:14,403 --> 00:06:15,843
something unexpected went wrong

182
00:06:15,843 --> 00:06:18,883
usually, it's an exception in your code that

183
00:06:18,883 --> 00:06:20,913
nothing caught in most real setups, a

184
00:06:20,913 --> 00:06:24,333
request first hits a proxy, like an NGINX or

185
00:06:24,333 --> 00:06:27,213
a load balancer, and the proxy passes it to

186
00:06:27,213 --> 00:06:30,483
your app 502 Bad Gateway means that the

187
00:06:30,483 --> 00:06:32,983
proxy got an invalid response from your app

188
00:06:32,983 --> 00:06:34,973
or couldn't get a proper response from it at

189
00:06:34,973 --> 00:06:37,923
all 504 Gateway Timeout means that the proxy

190
00:06:37,923 --> 00:06:39,843
waited for your app, and your app took too

191
00:06:39,843 --> 00:06:41,203
long to answer

192
00:06:41,203 --> 00:06:43,093
A very slow database query can actually

193
00:06:43,093 --> 00:06:44,003
cause this one

194
00:06:44,003 --> 00:06:45,963
503 Service Unavailable means the server

195
00:06:45,963 --> 00:06:48,163
cannot handle requests at the moment, and

196
00:06:48,163 --> 00:06:50,137
that situation is temporary

197
00:06:50,137 --> 00:06:51,477
It might be that the server is overloaded,

198
00:06:51,477 --> 00:06:53,447
or maybe the server is down for maintenance

199
00:06:53,447 --> 00:06:56,147
and there are some common HTTP status code

200
00:06:56,147 --> 00:06:58,017
mistakes in API that people make

201
00:06:58,017 --> 00:07:00,537
the first one, common one, is returning 200

202
00:07:00,537 --> 00:07:01,397
OK for everything

203
00:07:01,397 --> 00:07:03,077
if your request fails and your API still

204
00:07:03,077 --> 00:07:05,047
returns 200 OK, with something like error or

205
00:07:05,047 --> 00:07:06,857
something went wrong inside the body, your

206
00:07:06,857 --> 00:07:08,297
front end only sees 200

207
00:07:08,297 --> 00:07:09,967
your front end sees 200 and is like, OK,

208
00:07:09,967 --> 00:07:10,857
yeah, book saved

209
00:07:10,857 --> 00:07:13,257
your monitoring also counts it as a success

210
00:07:13,257 --> 00:07:15,897
so you need to send the actual status code

211
00:07:15,897 --> 00:07:17,617
and then put the details in the body

212
00:07:17,617 --> 00:07:19,287
the second mistake that people make with

213
00:07:19,287 --> 00:07:21,667
HTTP status code is using 500 for things

214
00:07:21,667 --> 00:07:23,207
that the clients got wrong

215
00:07:23,207 --> 00:07:25,167
for example, say someone says, GET book

216
00:07:25,167 --> 00:07:28,143
9999, and you use find instead of findOrFail

217
00:07:28,143 --> 00:07:29,743
so the book comes back as null, and then

218
00:07:29,743 --> 00:07:31,903
your code tries to read the title from null,

219
00:07:31,903 --> 00:07:33,893
and then it crashes, and the client gets a

220
00:07:33,893 --> 00:07:37,183
500 the right answer is a 404, not a 500,

221
00:07:37,183 --> 00:07:38,863
because the book does not exist

222
00:07:38,863 --> 00:07:40,463
in this scenario, a 500 is the wrong code,

223
00:07:40,463 --> 00:07:42,543
because 500 says that the server is broken

224
00:07:42,543 --> 00:07:43,783
meanwhile, the server is not broken

225
00:07:43,783 --> 00:07:45,583
that can just set off an alarm for nothing

226
00:07:45,583 --> 00:07:48,623
the third mistake is mixing 401 and 403

227
00:07:48,623 --> 00:07:51,333
if you return 403 when the user's token has

228
00:07:51,333 --> 00:07:52,773
expired, your front end doesn't send them

229
00:07:52,773 --> 00:07:54,583
back to the login, which is what your front

230
00:07:54,583 --> 00:07:55,143
end should do

231
00:07:55,143 --> 00:07:56,623
so they just, they are stuck

232
00:07:56,623 --> 00:07:59,303
the fourth one is leaking stack traces in

233
00:07:59,303 --> 00:08:00,343
your 500 response

234
00:08:00,343 --> 00:08:03,033
in Laravel, for example, if APP_DEBUG is

235
00:08:03,033 --> 00:08:05,793
true and something crashes, the JSON error

236
00:08:05,793 --> 00:08:08,093
response includes the exception message,

237
00:08:08,093 --> 00:08:10,543
your file path, the line number, the full

238
00:08:10,543 --> 00:08:12,863
stack trace that's useful for you as the

239
00:08:12,863 --> 00:08:14,993
engineer in production, however, if you show

240
00:08:14,993 --> 00:08:16,333
that, you are showing someone your folder

241
00:08:16,333 --> 00:08:17,543
structure and how your code runs

242
00:08:17,543 --> 00:08:20,313
so APP_DEBUG should always be false in

243
00:08:20,313 --> 00:08:22,917
production, with the body of your 500

244
00:08:22,917 --> 00:08:24,503
response just saying Server Error

245
00:08:24,503 --> 00:08:26,103
that's all your users need to know

246
00:08:26,103 --> 00:08:29,233
and one last HTTP status code, just for the

247
00:08:29,233 --> 00:08:30,043
fun of it

248
00:08:30,043 --> 00:08:31,703
418, I'm a teapot

249
00:08:31,703 --> 00:08:32,433
bye
